# AIBS Assignment 1: Report Writing with AI

After comparing the AI-generated articles, we noticed that AI can create
convincing arguments for different regions depending on how the question
is asked.

In our article about the United States, the US was presented as the global
AI leader because of its strong position in computing infrastructure,
investment, AI companies and frontier models. At the same time, the article
showed that China is becoming a stronger competitor.

## What we noticed

We noticed a similar pattern when comparing our three articles about the
Netherlands. None of the articles stated that the Netherlands dominates AI
globally, but all three presented it as an important AI player.

The articles agreed that the Netherlands is strong in universities,
research and AI talent. However, they also mentioned limitations in
computing power, investment and the development of large AI models.

The articles focused on different strengths. One focused on talent and
agriculture, another on academic research and responsible AI, while the
third focused on ASML, semiconductors and digital infrastructure.

## Conclusion

The comparison shows that AI-generated articles can sound convincing while
giving different conclusions. Some claims were also made without clear
supporting sources.

Therefore, simply asking AI which country or region leads in AI is not a
reliable research method. AI claims should be compared and checked using
reliable and independent sources.

AIBS Assignment 2 Search Strategies & Quality Criteria
1. Research question
For this assignment, the following question was selected:
How does geopolitical competition influence the development of AI in Europe?
This question focuses on issues such as access to semiconductors, computing power, technological dependence and government policies. 

2. Two search strategies
Search strategy 1 Broad search
First, a broad search was conducted using keywords such as:
“AI geopolitics Europe”, “AI sovereignty Europe” and “AI-semiconductor export controls”.
The purpose of this search was to identify the main themes and organisations involved. The search showed that semiconductor access, computing infrastructure and technological sovereignty are important topics in the geopolitical development of AI.
Search strategy 2 Authoritative sources
The second strategy focused specifically on reliable organisations, using searches such as:
Site: https://digital-strategy.ec.europa.eu/en/library/communication-european-tech-sovereignty-accompanied-eu-open-source-strategy  
Site: https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2026-ai-index-report/policy-and-governance  
Site: https://media.bis.gov/press-release/commerce-strengthens-restrictions-advanced-computing-semiconductors-enhance-foundry-due-diligence-prevent  
This resulted in sources from the European Commission, Stanford University and the U.S. Bureau of Industry and Security.
The European Commission states that Europe wants to reduce its dependence on non-EU suppliers for technologies such as semiconductors, cloud infrastructure and AI. Stanford's 2026 AI Index also describes AI sovereignty as an increasingly important part of national AI policy. 




3. Source assessment
The sources were assessed based on authority, relevance, recency and evidence.
The European Commission (2026) was assessed as a high-quality source. It is a recent and primary European policy source and is directly relevant to the topic of AI and technological sovereignty.
The Stanford AI Index (2026) was also assessed as a high-quality source. It is research-based, recent and provides international data about developments in AI.
The U.S. Bureau of Industry and Security (2025) was assessed as a high-quality source because it is a primary government source about semiconductor export controls.
The U.S. government source is useful for understanding how export controls are used in geopolitical technology competition. However, it represents the perspective and policy objectives of the U.S. government. Therefore, it should be compared with other sources to create a more balanced analysis.
 
4. AI-analysis
AI was used to compare the sources and identify similarities and differences.
The sources show that AI-development is increasingly connected to geopolitics. Countries and regions want more control over important technologies such as chips, computing infrastructure and AI-models. Europe is trying to become less dependent on foreign technology providers, while U.S.-export controls show that access to advanced semiconductors can also be restricted for geopolitical reasons. 
AI helped identify these connections, but the conclusions were checked against the original sources. AI should therefore support the analysis, while the researcher remains responsible for checking whether the conclusions are actually supported by the evidence.

